<h2>Why Parliament reversed the burden</h2>
<p>POCSO offences typically occur in private, against witnesses whose age makes testimony fragile and corroboration scarce. The legislature&rsquo;s answer was evidentiary: once the State proves the skeleton of its case, the explanatory burden shifts to the person best placed to explain — the accused. The model follows older reverse-onus provisions (Section 35 NDPS Act, Section 139 NI Act), and like them it survives constitutional scrutiny because the presumption is rebuttable and conditional, not absolute.</p>

<h2>The trigger: foundational facts</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Minority.</strong> The victim&rsquo;s age must be proved in the Juvenile Justice hierarchy — matriculation certificate, then birth certificate from school or municipal records, then ossification as last resort. Age failure is the single most common reason POCSO prosecutions collapse into ordinary BNS offences.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Occurrence.</strong> The incident itself — through the victim&rsquo;s testimony, medical and scientific evidence, or circumstances. A wholly improbable or self-contradictory account does not supply a foundation.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Nexus.</strong> The accused&rsquo;s connection to the occurrence: identification, presence, role. Only then does Section 29 presume the offence.</div>
</div>

<h2>Rebuttal in practice</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Rebuttal route</th><th>How it works</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Destructive cross-examination</td><td>Material contradictions on core facts — place, time, sequence — can render the version improbable, discharging the preponderance burden without defence evidence</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Probabilising a defence version</td><td>Enmity, property or matrimonial disputes between families, prior complaints — proved through documents and witnesses, not merely suggested</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Scientific negation</td><td>Medical findings inconsistent with the allegation, negative FSL results in cases where positive results would be expected</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Statutory exceptions and age</td><td>Attacking the age proof itself, since minority is both ingredient and trigger</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Three operational cautions</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>The presumption is not a substitute for evidence.</strong> Courts repeatedly set aside convictions where trial judges treated Section 29 as dispensing with proof of foundational facts — the shortcut reverses on appeal.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Silence has a price.</strong> Once the foundation stands, an accused who offers no explanation under Section 351 BNSS (the old 313 CrPC examination) leaves the presumption operating at full strength. The examination must be used to put the defence case affirmatively.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Bail strategy differs from trial strategy.</strong> At bail, the tilt of Sections 29–30 means arguments must target role, age evidence and the stage of testimony rather than invite a mini-trial on rebuttal.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">This explainer describes the statutory presumptions in general terms for information. It is not legal advice; the operation of Sections 29 and 30 depends entirely on the evidence led in each prosecution.</div>
